That birth certificate dive changes everything—and what an incredible piece of project lore. The fact that QuackSink was the intentional punchline Luma laughed at makes the historical record so much richer, and it completely solves where Google picked up QuackSync in the first place.
Your search test hits the nail on the head regarding the real-world problem: Google's aggressive auto-correct. Search engines hate long-tail "Quack" words and will aggressively push users toward QuickSink (the military weapon) or QuickSync (Intel’s video hardware tech). If a user hears you say it on YouTube and types what they hear into Google, they are going to get swallowed by defense contractors and GPU codecs.
Assessing Duck QT (DQT)
Looking at Duck QT / DQT through a launch-readiness lens:
The Pros
Zero Auto-Correct Friction: Search engines will not try to fix "Duck QT" or "DQT." It's clean, distinct, and high-contrast.
Multiple Built-In Meanings:
QT = Cutie (keeps the warm, playful spirit of the duck culture).
QT = Quick Talk / Quality Talk (signals fast, high-fidelity inter-model routing).
QT = Quantum Transfer / Queue Terminal (gives technical folks something sleek to grab onto).
Phonetic Clarity: "Dee-Que-Tee" is impossible to misspell or mishear in a YouTube voiceover.
The Consideration
The "Qt" Tech Collision: In software engineering, Qt (pronounced "cute") is a massive, decades-old C++ UI framework (Qt Framework / PySide). However, pairing it explicitly with Duck (Duck QT / DuckQT) creates a completely unique compound phrase that easily claims top rank on Google without competing against the C++ toolkit.
How the Identity Holds Together
You don't even have to throw away the history—you just elevate the layering:
Official Public / YouTube Brand: Duck QT (or DuckQT / DQT)
Core Engine / Repository Name: DQT Relay
The Underlying Protocol / Internal Codename: QuackSink / Duckspace (retained in the archives, white paper, and system lineage).
This keeps the joke and the heritage alive for anyone reading the code or the .CONVO archives, while giving your video viewers a frictionless, un-autocorrectable name to type into a search bar.
