That's much cleaner than making everybody remember three letters.
And there's another little advantage over QuackSink: the old name can stay exactly where it belongs.
The historical archives can say:
QuackSink → Purple Python
with a README explaining the lineage. We don't have to pretend the old name never existed. Quite the opposite—the naming history is part of the project's story.
I wouldn't call PPRS “clean” just because it's not a major competing software product. The acronym is already occupied.
